   95:       write by the first refusal would be overwritten before the read) -- and no other cell, width, selector or
   96:       COMMAND value (NOT CLAIMED (8))
   97:   an accepted selector is stored -- 0 and 7 at every width (W, A), every selector at one width (A); latch unset (A, W)
   98:       and set (V's fills and Z's selections, after their first refusals)
   99: Beyond the effects: the store of an out-of-range value, clamped, masked or reset; the compare narrowed or signed (bit
  100: 63 alone, bits 32-33, 32-bit truncation) or made to ignore any set of the bits it tests, at any width (B: every
  101: single-bit value is refused); a boundary slip at 8; an announce on an accepted selector; an announce
  102: demoted to debug() (F); an announce suppressed above or below any threshold, or on the sign bit (the first refusals
  103: run from 8 to all-ones); and the pre-#549 process exit on DATA.
  104: NOT CLAIMED (disclosed).  (0) An edit conditioned on TWO axes at once -- a joint cell such as "refuse 9 only by sb
  105: after PORT=3", "`` sets bit 32 except at selector 5", or THE LATCH CROSSED WITH ONE BIT (while `` is clear the
  106: compare ignores bit k) -- is caught only where a row happens to hold that pair; the enumeration above is one-axis by
  107: design, and closing every pair is the unbounded growth the carrier's floor rules refuse.  The latch x bit joint is held
  108: at bits 3, 8 and 32 only (the first refusals 8, 0x100 and 0x100000005; all-ones decides no bit), so 58 bits are open
  109: at latch-clear (4-7, 9-31, 33-63): measured 117/117 at bits 4 and 40 (opus549_measured/f2v6/TABLE_f2v6.out).  What it
  110: lets through is a guest's FIRST out-of-range selector, once per device: below bit 32 the value is stored and the next
  111: DATA access ends the process in the kept range check; from bit 32 up it is truncated onto a LEGAL port silently --
  112: 2^40 selects port 0 and the next DATA write runs as a command (WITNESS.out) -- the pre-#549 defect, for one write.  Not
  113: owed by the floor, whose generating set for `` is below/at/past the bound, held at both latch states; the close, one
  114: fresh device per bit at a second anchor (61 sessions, 9.7 s on the fix: TABLE_U.out), is filed as ``.  One pair is
  115: held and is recorded as a pair, not as a class: COMMAND non-zero with the latch set, before a PORT read (R's second
  116: refusal; V's and Y's later ones) -- in the 94-row probe R was its only holder (the Opus closing-F2 seat,
  117: J_ref_cmdlatch).  (1) A process-global latch instead of the per-instance field, and an announce conditioned on the
  118: machine's serial number.  The probe's `-E testmips` session has ONE fbctrl.  A second instance IS reachable without
  119: adding a device -- two testmips machines in one process from a config file, `gxemul @config` -- and there the
  120: process-global latch reds (measured by the Opus F2 seat, opus549_measured/f2/TABLE_twoinst.out), but that is a new
  121: launcher this probe does not use: filed as `fbctrltwoinst`.  The latch is per instance by construction, a struct
  122: member, untested across two machines.  (2) Any edit of the DATA arm's kept range check -- deleting it, deleting only
  123: its `exit(1)`, weakening `>=` to `>`, or swapping its operand -- is unreachable while the PORT arm refuses, because no
  124: out-of-range selector is ever stored; the check is KEPT as a tripwire.  It exits before it indexes, so an index of 8
  125: cannot reach `warned_port` while it stands, and `port[8]` would be undefined behaviour, not a defined alias of the
  126: latch.  (3) An exception for one value no row writes (``), or for a CLASS the rows never present: the low three bits
  127: (every refused value's residue mod 8 is 0, 1, 3, 5 or 7, never 2, 4 or 6 -- `` scores the full count, and a wide
  128: value with such a residue is accepted and truncated onto a legal port silently, 0x100000006 to port 6); an accept-side
  129: class (every value with exactly four bits set: 0x700000001 is accepted silently as port 1 -- not always the exit); a
  130: latch that re-arms with a period of 108 refusals or more (107 re-announces on B's 108th refusal and reds the count
  131: row; 108 would re-announce on a 109th no session makes -- measured); an announce suppressed or mangled only for one
  132: value no session refuses first (``) or only for a class no first refusal belongs to (values whose bits all lie in
  133: 9..31, say); an announce demoted to debug() for some values but not F's 9 -- only a free-running session tells them
  134: apart; and A REFUSAL THAT WRITES THE CONSTANT EQUAL TO A VALUE'S ONLY ANCHOR: the rows read "keeps the previous
  135: selector" against the stored-int oracle, so `` reads as a keep.  Sixty refused values are observed at exactly one
  136: previous selector -- 58 single bits at 5, 0xffffffff at 5, 0x100000005 at 3 (SIM_ANCHORS.out) -- and each admits a
  137: refusal-time write of that selector (measured); bits 3, 4 and 8 are dual-anchored (V, X7 at 7) and red.  A second
  138: anchor for a value closes its constant form; a replaced anchor moves it (B's sd group at 7 opens `` on bits 32 and
  139: 34-62); no finite row set closes "except value k".  (A threshold is not in this class, nor is a range of bits the
  140: COMPARE ignores: see "Beyond the effects".)  (4) Equivalent edits, THIRTEEN, each measured at the full score
  141: (table5/TABLE.out, table6/TABLE.out): storing `(uint32_t) idata`, `(int) idata` or `idata & 7` (only 0..7 reach that
  142: store, and each compiles to the fix's own binary), reordering the struct's fields, masking the PORT read with 7 (the
  143: selector is always 0..7), a PORT read that skips its load at selector 0 (odata starts at 0), a latch set to any
  144: nonzero value in place of 1, the bound written `idata > 7`, `(idata & ~7ULL) != 0` or with the literal 8 (each equal
  145: to `idata >= DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS` on every uint64_t), the direction tested as `writeflag != MEM_WRITE` (the arm receives
  146: only MEM_READ and MEM_WRITE), the latch tested as `warned_port != 1` (it holds only 0 or 1), and the announce
  147: additionally requiring a nonzero idata (a refused value is never 0).  (5) `printf` in place of `fatal()`, or the
  148: announce without its trailing newline: indistinguishable in THIS invocation's output.  (6) Narrowing the DATA path's
  149: widths, and the device's OTHER two process-ending calls -- fbctrl_command()'s default (any command other than 0-2,
  150: including the documented but unimplemented FILL/COPY) and the access function's default (any offset other than
  151: 0x00/0x10): filed as `fbctrlcmdkill` and `fbctrloffkill`.  (7) Edits to this instrument's own logic are reviewed, not
  152: rowed.  (8) The data cells' VALUES are not an axis the rows enumerate.  An edit conditioned on a cell's value
  153: (`port[COLOR_B] == 0`, `port[Y1] != 0`) is caught only where a row's refusal or read holds a triggering state, and the
  154: states held are: every cell 0 (F, W, X, Z, B), port 5 at 0x2a (P, K, R), X1 at 123 with COMMAND 2 (V's first refusal),
  155: ports 1-7 at 0x11*p with COMMAND 2 (V's later refusals and the PORT read right after them), and COMMAND 1 (Y).  The
  156: cells-alone effect is claimed only as listed: no cell is read back after an `sh` refusal or after a refusal at
  157: previous selector 0, 2, 3, 4 or 6, and after a refusal at previous 5 the only cells read back are port 5's under
  158: COMMAND 0 (K) and COMMAND's under COMMAND 1 (Y, its second refusal only).
  159: LIMITS OF A ROW.  Session P re-selects PORT=5 before each refusal, so a refused selector cannot carry over into the
  160: next row WHEN THE LEGAL SELECTION LANDS; an edit that also refuses legal selections defeats the re-select, and later
  161: rows then inherit its value (M_acclatch).  Every read-back is taken after its session ends, so a session that dies
  162: part-way blanks the rows it had already passed (M_acc4: session A died at step 5 and every accept row read `-`).  And
  163: session A's "accept selector 0 written by sw" cannot see an IGNORED store -- the selector is already 0 at power-on --
  164: so the cell "0 by sw is accepted" is held by W's row after PORT=5, not by A's.  Session B reads PORT once per width, so
  165: each of its rows says that SOME single-bit value of that width got through, not which: it prints the last value that
  166: GOT THROUGH, which names the bit for k <= 31 (2^k; INT_MIN prints 0xffffffff80000000) and reads 0 for bits 32-63.  B's
  167: "keeps 5" is verified against the stored-int oracle only: a refusal that WRITES 5 reads as a keep (NOT CLAIMED (3)).  B
  168: depends on the emulated `` executing -- the encoding is pinned and the disassembly control checks the word, not the
  169: doubling; table 6's width-differentiated reds (bit 9 red at sh/sw/sd and never sb, bit 16 and up never sh, bit 32 and
  170: up never sw) are the evidence each 2^k reached the compare.  After a refusal, the DATA accesses made without a fresh
  171: selector are K's read, K's write and R's write; R's DATA read re-selects port 5 first.  Every refusal row rests on
  172: `fatal()` printing and returning on this path (src/core/debugmsg.c's `fatal()`: no early-out and no exit) -- twelve
  173: refusals in P and 108 in B.
  174: 
  175: Every stepped word is compared with the emulator's own step line (the hand-assembled-encoding trap).
  176: Output: one row per check, then FBCTRLP_CONTROL=OK|FAILED, FBCTRLP_RESULT=n/t, FBCTRLP_DONE.  The exit status is 0
  177: only when the controls are OK and every row is green: a red row is a nonzero exit, not only a crash.
  178: usage: fbctrl_port_probe.py <gxemul-binary>
  179: """
  180: import os
  181: import pty
  182: import re
  183: import select
  184: import signal
  185: import sys
  186: import time
  187: 
  188: BIN = sys.argv[1]
  189: 
  190: STUBADDR = 0xffffffff80100000
  191: CODE = 0xffffffff80100020
  192: FBCTRL = 0xffffffffb2f00000
  193: 
  194: ONCE_RE = re.compile(r"\[ fbctrl: port number 0x[0-9a-f]+ is out of range; the selector is unchanged \(once\) \]")
  195: #  The refused value, captured whole: "0x8" can match only 0x8, never 0x80 (the digits run to " is").
  196: VAL_RE = re.compile(r"\[ fbctrl: port number (0x[0-9a-f]+) is out of range; the selector is unchanged \(once\) \]")
  197: 
  198: REG = dict(zr=0, v0=2, v1=3, a0=4, a1=5, a2=6, a3=7, t0=8, t1=9, t2=10, t3=11, t4=12, t5=13, t6=14, t7=15,
  199:            s0=16, s1=17, s2=18, s3=19, s4=20, s5=21, s6=22, s7=23, t8=24, t9=25)
  200: OPS = dict(sb=0x28, sh=0x29, sw=0x2b, sd=0x3f, lb=0x20, lh=0x21, lw=0x23, ld=0x37)
  201: LOADS = ("lb", "lh", "lw", "ld")
  202: 
  203: 
  204: def ins(op, rt, off, base="t0"):
  205:     if op == "dsll":                                     # SPECIAL dsll rt,rt,off: rd == rt, the shift in sa
  206:         return (REG[rt] << 16) | (REG[rt] << 11) | ((off & 31) << 6) | 0x38
  207:     return (OPS[op] << 26) | (REG[base] << 21) | (REG[rt] << 16) | (off & 0xffff)
  208: 
  209: 
  210: def text(op, rt, off, base="t0"):
  211:     if op == "dsll":
  212:         return "dsll %s,%s,%d" % (rt, rt, off)
  213:     return "%s %s,%d(%s)" % (op, rt, off, base)
  214: 
  215: 
  216: ANCHOR = (0x0f << 26) | (REG["t7"] << 16)                   # lui t7,0 -- the breakpoint anchor, never executed
  217: #  Pinned independently of the helper: a wrong register field would otherwise pass silently.
  218: assert ins("sw", "t1", 0) == 0xad090000
  219: assert ins("lw", "t2", 0x10) == 0x8d0a0010
  220: assert ins("sd", "t5", 0) == 0xfd0d0000
  221: assert ins("sb", "t8", 0) == 0xa1180000
  222: assert ins("sh", "t9", 0) == 0xa5190000
  223: assert ins("ld", "s3", 0) == 0xdd130000
  224: assert ins("sb", "zr", 0) == 0xa1000000
  225: assert ins("lb", "a1", 0) == 0x81050000
  226: assert ins("lh", "a2", 0) == 0x85060000
  227: assert ANCHOR == 0x3c0f0000
  228: assert ins("dsll", "t1", 1) == 0x00094878
  229: assert ins("dsll", "t4", 1) == 0x000c6078
  230: 
  231: 
  232: def stub_path():
  233:     p = "/tmp/gx_fbctrl549p_%d.bin" % os.getpid()
  234:     with open(p, "wb") as f:
  235:         f.write(b"\x00\x00\x00\x00")
  236:     return p
  237: 
  238: 
  239: def session(label, prog, regs, reads, stub, free_run=False):
  240:     """prog: list of (op, rt, off, tag).  Steps each (or free-runs to an anchor after the last), then prints reads.
  241:     Returns dict: status, exit, badis, vals, once_after (tag -> cumulative (once) count right after that step), buf."""
  242:     pid, fd = pty.fork()
  243:     if pid == 0:
  244:         os.execvp(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-E", "testmips", "-M", "64", "0x%x:%s" % (STUBADDR, stub)])
  245:         os._exit(127)
  246:     buf = ""
  247:     dead = [False]
  248: 
  249:     def rd(t=0.4):
  250:         nonlocal buf
  251:         r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], t)
  252:         if fd not in r:
  253:             return True
  254:         try:
  255:             d = os.read(fd, 65536)
  256:         except OSError:
  257:             dead[0] = True
  258:             return False
  259:         if not d:
  260:             dead[0] = True
  261:             return False
  262:         buf += d.decode("latin1", "replace")
  263:         return True
  264: 
  265:     def wait(mark=0, echo=None, timeout=60):
  266:         t = time.time()
  267:         while time.time() - t < timeout:
  268:             if not rd():
  269:                 return False
  270:             resp = buf[mark:]
  271:             if echo is not None and echo not in resp:
  272:                 continue
  273:             if len(buf) > mark and resp.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>"):
  274:                 return True
  275:         return False
  276: 
  277:     def send(x, timeout=60):
  278:         b = (x + "\n").encode("latin1")
  279:         _mark = len(buf)
  280:         n = 0
  281:         try:
  282:             while n < len(b):
  283:                 n += os.write(fd, b[n:])
  284:         except OSError:
  285:             dead[0] = True
  286:             return False
  287:         return wait(mark=_mark, echo=x if x else None, timeout=timeout)
  288: 
  289:     words = [ins(op, rt, off) for op, rt, off, _t in prog] + [ANCHOR]
  290:     status, badis, vals, once_after, steps = "?", [], {}, {}, 0
  291:     if not wait(timeout=120):
  292:         status = "NO-PROMPT"
  293:     else:
  294:         for i, w in enumerate(words):
  295:             send("put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (CODE + 4 * i, w))
  296:         for name, val in regs:
  297:             send("%s=0x%x" % (name, val & 0xffffffffffffffff))
  298:         send("pc=0x%x" % CODE)
  299:         status = "SURVIVED"
  300:         if free_run:
  301:             send("breakpoint add 0x%x" % (CODE + 4 * len(prog)))
  302:             mark = len(buf)
  303:             b = b"continue\n"
  304:             try:
  305:                 os.write(fd, b)
  306:             except OSError:
  307:                 dead[0] = True
  308:             if not wait(mark=mark, timeout=60) or dead[0]:
  309:                 status = "HOST-DIED" if dead[0] else "NO-BREAK"
  310:             elif ("%016x" % (CODE + 4 * len(prog))) not in buf[mark:] and \
  311:                     ("%08x" % ((CODE + 4 * len(prog)) & 0xffffffff)) not in buf[mark:]:
  312:                 status = "NO-BREAK"
  313:             else:
  314:                 steps = len(prog)
  315:                 once_after["free"] = len(ONCE_RE.findall(buf))
  316:         else:
  317:             for i, (op, rt, off, tag) in enumerate(prog):
  318:                 mark = len(buf)
  319:                 if not send("step 1", timeout=30) or dead[0]:
  320:                     status = "HOST-DIED"
  321:                     break
  322:                 steps += 1
  323:                 seg = buf[mark:]
  324:                 w = words[i]
  325:                 m = re.search(r"%016x:?\s+%08x\s+([^\r\n<]+)" % (CODE + 4 * i, w), seg)
  326:                 got = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", m.group(1)).strip() if m else "(no step line)"
  327:                 if not got.startswith(text(op, rt, off)):
  328:                     badis.append("+0x%02x planted 0x%08x -> %r, expected %r" % (4 * i, w, got, text(op, rt, off)))
  329:                 if tag:
  330:                     once_after[tag] = len(ONCE_RE.findall(buf))
  331:         if status == "SURVIVED":
  332:             for name in reads:
  333:                 got = None
  334:                 for _ in range(2):
  335:                     mark = len(buf)
  336:                     if not send("print %s" % name, timeout=30) or dead[0]:
  337:                         break
  338:                     m = re.search(r"(?m)^0x([0-9a-fA-F]+)\s*$", buf[mark:])
  339:                     if m:
  340:                         got = int(m.group(1), 16)
  341:                         break
  342:                 vals[name] = got
  343:     try:
  344:         os.write(fd, b"quit\n")
  345:     except OSError:
  346:         pass
  347:     t1 = time.time()
  348:     while time.time() - t1 < 20.0:
  349:         if not rd(0.1):
  350:             break
  351:     try:
  352:         os.close(fd)
  353:     except OSError:
  354:         pass
  355:     rpid, st = 0, None
  356:     t1 = time.time()
  357:     while rpid == 0 and time.time() - t1 < 10.0:
  358:         try:
  359:             rpid, st = os.waitpid(pid, os.WNOHANG)
  360:         except OSError:
  361:             break
  362:         if rpid == 0:
  363:             time.sleep(0.05)
  364:     if rpid == 0:
  365:         try:
  366:             os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
  367:             _, st = os.waitpid(pid, 0)
  368:         except OSError:
  369:             st = None
  370:     code = os.WEXITSTATUS(st) if st is not None and os.WIFEXITED(st) else None
  371:     print("  session %-2s %-9s steps=%d/%d exit=%s once=%d" % (label, status, steps, len(prog), code,
  372:                                                               len(ONCE_RE.findall(buf))))
  373:     for b in badis:
  374:         print("      DISASSEMBLY MISMATCH: %s" % b)
  375:     return {"status": status, "exit": code, "badis": badis, "vals": vals, "once_after": once_after,
  376:             "steps": steps, "buf": buf, "n": len(prog)}
  377: 
  378: 
  379: RESULTS = []
  380: 
  381: 
  382: def row(name, got, want, note=""):
  383:     ok = got == want
  384:     RESULTS.append(ok)
  385:     print("  %-4s %-48s got=%-20s want=%-20s %s" % ("ok" if ok else "FAIL", name, got, want, note))
  386:     return ok
  387: 
  388: 
  389: def hx(v):
  390:     return "-" if v is None else "0x%x" % v
  391: 
  392: 
  393: def first_val(S):
  394:     """the value the FIRST announce of a session names, or '-'"""
  395:     m = VAL_RE.findall(S["buf"])
  396:     return m[0] if m else "-"
  397: 
  398: 
  399: M64 = 0xffffffffffffffff
  400: 
  401: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- sessions
  402: PROG_C = [("sw", "t1", 0, None), ("sw", "t3", 16, None), ("lw", "t4", 16, None), ("lw", "t5", 0, None),
  403:           ("sw", "t6", 0, None), ("sw", "t7", 16, None),               # PORT=1 (X1), X1 = 123
  404:           ("sw", "s1", 0, None), ("sw", "t7", 16, None),               # PORT=2 (Y1), Y1 = 123
  405:           ("sw", "s0", 0, None), ("sw", "s1", 16, None),               # PORT=0 (COMMAND), DATA=2: GET_RESOLUTION
  406:           ("sw", "t6", 0, None), ("lw", "s2", 16, None),               # read X1
  407:           ("sw", "s1", 0, None), ("lw", "s3", 16, None)]               # read Y1
  408: REGS_C = [("t0", FBCTRL), ("t1", 5), ("t3", 0x2a), ("t6", 1), ("t7", 123), ("s0", 0), ("s1", 2),
  409:           ("t4", 0xdead), ("t5", 0xdead), ("s2", 0xdead), ("s3", 0xdead)]
  410: 
  411: #  A: selector p written at a rotating width and read back through PORT into s<k> (poisoned 0xdead first, so a
  412: #  read that never ran cannot pass -- selector 0 would otherwise match a register's reset value); DATA value 0x11*p
  413: #  for p >= 1, read back through DATA INTO THE SELECTOR REGISTER, which holds p before the read, never 0x11*p.
  414: #  Port 0 is COMMAND: written with NOP (0, from zr) only.
  415: ACC = [(0, "sw"), (1, "sb"), (2, "sh"), (3, "sw"), (4, "sd"), (5, "sb"), (6, "sh"), (7, "sd")]
  416: SEL_REG = ["t1", "t2", "t3", "t4", "t5", "t6", "t7", "t8"]           # the selector values
  417: VAL_REG = ["zr", "a0", "a1", "a2", "a3", "v0", "v1", "t9"]           # the DATA values (port 0 gets zr = NOP)
  418: PORT_RB = ["s0", "s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6", "s7"]           # PORT read-backs
  419: PROG_A, REGS_A = [], [("t0", FBCTRL)]
  420: for k, (p, w) in enumerate(ACC):
  421:     REGS_A.append((SEL_REG[k], p))
  422:     REGS_A.append((PORT_RB[k], 0xdead))
  423:     if k:
  424:         REGS_A.append((VAL_REG[k], 0x11 * p))
  425:     PROG_A.append((w, SEL_REG[k], 0, None))                            # PORT = p at width w
  426:     PROG_A.append(("lw", PORT_RB[k], 0, None))                         # read PORT
  427:     PROG_A.append(("sw", VAL_REG[k], 16, None))                        # DATA = 0x11*p (NOP for port 0)
  428: #  the DATA read-backs, one per port 1..7: re-select p, read DATA into the selector register
  429: for k, (p, w) in enumerate(ACC):
  430:     if k:
  431:         PROG_A.append(("sw", SEL_REG[k], 0, None))
  432:         PROG_A.append(("lw", SEL_REG[k], 16, None))
  433: READS_A = PORT_RB + SEL_REG[1:]
  434: 
  435: #  P: previous selector 5, then nine refusals with read-backs (the FIRST is 8), two count-only refusals, and the
  436: #  twelfth after PORT=7.  The count is sampled after the twelfth, so a latch re-armed every N refusals, N <= 11, reds.
  437: REFUSE = [("sw", "t2", 8, "8 (sw)"), ("sw", "t1", 9, "9 (sw)"), ("sw", "t3", 16, "16 (sw)"),
  438:           ("sd", "t4", 0x200000000, "0x200000000 (sd)"), ("sd", "t5", 0x100000003, "0x100000003 (sd)"),
  439:           ("sd", "t6", M64, "all-ones (sd)"), ("sw", "t7", 0xffffffff, "all-ones (sw)"),
  440:           ("sb", "t8", 0xff, "0xff (sb)"), ("sh", "t9", 0x100, "0x100 (sh)")]
  441: #  16 by sb and by sh, each read back into a register whose own value (0x200000000, 0x100000003) is spent by then and
  442: #  can never read as 5 -- so an ignored read, or a session that died, reds the row too.
  443: EXTRA = [("sb", "t3", "t4", "16 (sb)"), ("sh", "t3", "t5", "16 (sh)")]
  444: READ_P = ["s0", "s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6", "s7", "v0"]
  445: PROG_P = [("sw", "a0", 0, None), ("sw", "a1", 16, None)]
  446: REGS_P = [("t0", FBCTRL), ("a0", 5), ("a1", 0x2a), ("a2", 7)]
