VERDICT: CONFIRM WITH CHANGES

The C fix is correct **by reading**. `idata` is `uint64_t`; the comparison precedes the selector assignment; only values 0–7 reach that assignment. Initialization zeroes both selector and per-device latch. `fatal()` prints and returns, and nothing resets the latch. The two supplied diffs match the working files, and all three MD5 pins match. [Fix](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_fbctrl.c:107), [initialization](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_fbctrl.c:170), [fatal](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/debugmsg.c:384).

I ran no emulator or builds. **Measurements below are recorded measurements**, checked against their artifacts. Hash comparisons, AST comparisons, counts, and source conclusions are my read-only checks.

I uphold the flagship’s **D1 disclosure / D2 disclosure / no mandatory v7** disposition. The bounded rule makes the relational comparison’s generating set below/at/past the bound, crossed with the latch state. Session B’s additional single-bit coverage does not automatically promise every bit at both latch states. The earlier #548 ruling supports that interpretation independently of the newly added clarification. Restoring the original defect is consequential, but does not itself change that rule’s classification. [Rule](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/CLAUDE.md:1450), [#548 precedent](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/CHANGELOG.md:96).

I **overturn the “once per device / for one write” consequence bound**. The measured D1 variant leaves the latch clear when a selector escapes. Its escape can therefore recur until an actual refusal sets the latch. The wide-value witness establishes silent truncation and command execution for one tested write; it does not establish a one-write maximum. This correction does not change my floor ruling.

The principal counts reconcile:

- Table 6 contains **297 edits: 267 red, 30 green**, with all 297 RED/GREEN predictions matching. Its thirteen equivalent edits score 117/117.
- All **61 single-bit variants**—60 `MKB` entries plus bit 8’s control—have the recorded B-width failures expected from their bits. The four width-conditioned variants fail their respective conditioned widths.
- The final probe log contains **16 sessions and 117 passing rows**. Gate 16 records **PASS, 26 checks, 18 seconds**.
- The last measuring pass contains **22 predicted edits**, all predictions matching, and **46 complete probe logs**. U records **61 successful sessions in 9.7 seconds** on the fix.
- The earlier table counts **62/73, 100/94, and 212/111**—edits/rows—also check.

